WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.255
Part three of lecture eight is a classic

00:00:04.353 --> 00:00:06.825
puzzle with two pointers.

00:00:06.825 --> 00:00:10.187
Here are two singly linked lists.

00:00:10.187 --> 00:00:13.647
List one is a1, a2, a3

00:00:13.647 --> 00:00:15.822
then c1 and c2.

00:00:15.822 --> 00:00:18.392
List two is b1, b2

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:21.853
and then the same c1 and c2.

00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:23.336
From c1 on

00:00:23.336 --> 00:00:26.598
the two lists share their nodes

00:00:26.598 --> 00:00:28.180
they intersect.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:32.816
The task is to find the first common node

00:00:32.816 --> 00:00:34.041
here c1.

00:00:34.041 --> 00:00:38.531
List one has M nodes, list two has N nodes

00:00:38.531 --> 00:00:41.491
and we want the answer fast

00:00:41.491 --> 00:00:44.450
with almost no extra memory.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.303
Notice the shape.

00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:48.475
Once two lists share a node

00:00:48.475 --> 00:00:50.871
they share everything after it

00:00:50.946 --> 00:00:52.892
because each node has only

00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:54.390
one next reference.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.028
So two intersecting lists look

00:00:57.105 --> 00:00:59.164
like a Y lying on its side.

00:00:59.164 --> 00:01:01.529
They can never look like an X

00:01:01.605 --> 00:01:03.817
after the meeting point there

00:01:03.894 --> 00:01:05.572
is only one way to go.

00:01:05.572 --> 00:01:07.860
The lists cannot split again.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.470
What does common mean here?

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.802
It means the same node object

00:01:12.802 --> 00:01:15.584
not a node that holds the same data.

00:01:15.584 --> 00:01:17.915
These are two different nodes

00:01:17.915 --> 00:01:20.246
and both hold the label c1.

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:20.923
In Java

00:01:20.998 --> 00:01:24.834
a == b on two references is true exactly

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:27.541
when they refer to the same object

00:01:27.541 --> 00:01:29.120
so here it is false.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.902
Comparing the labels would say true

00:01:31.902 --> 00:01:34.309
and that answer would be wrong.

00:01:34.309 --> 00:01:36.640
Always compare the references.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.192
Our node class is as small as it gets

00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:43.310
a string label and a next reference.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.188
Method one is the obvious one.

00:01:46.188 --> 00:01:48.640
Walk list one with a pointer a.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.559
For each a

00:01:49.559 --> 00:01:52.547
walk all of list two with a pointer b

00:01:52.547 --> 00:01:55.994
and check whether a and b are the same node.

00:01:56.071 --> 00:01:58.599
The first a that turns up in list

00:01:58.676 --> 00:02:00.898
two is the first common node

00:02:00.974 --> 00:02:02.813
because we try the nodes

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.498
of list one in order.

00:02:04.498 --> 00:02:06.873
A static counter, comparisons,

00:02:06.873 --> 00:02:08.252
counts the tests.

00:02:08.252 --> 00:02:11.010
If nothing matches, we return null.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.235
Let's run it.

00:02:13.235 --> 00:02:18.929
a starts at a1, and b walks b1, b2

00:02:18.929 --> 00:02:22.979
c1, c2: four comparisons

00:02:22.979 --> 00:02:24.750
and no match.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.226
a moves on to a2: four more.

00:02:28.226 --> 00:02:32.260
Then a3: four more, twelve so far.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.481
Now a is at c1.

00:02:34.481 --> 00:02:38.430
b tries b1, b2, and then c1

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.989
the same node.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:42.172
Fifteen comparisons

00:02:42.172 --> 00:02:44.770
and the answer is c1.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.869
In the worst case the common node

00:02:47.952 --> 00:02:50.534
is the last node of both lists

00:02:50.534 --> 00:02:52.617
or there is none at all

00:02:52.617 --> 00:02:56.032
and the inner loop runs M * N times.

00:02:56.032 --> 00:02:58.031
That is O(MN) time

00:02:58.031 --> 00:03:00.280
with constant extra space.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.396
First, a special case.

00:03:02.396 --> 00:03:05.468
Suppose the two lists have the same length.

00:03:05.538 --> 00:03:08.191
Then they also have the same number of

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.704
nodes before the first common node

00:03:10.774 --> 00:03:12.379
because the shared tail

00:03:12.449 --> 00:03:13.915
is the same for both.

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:16.289
So start one pointer at each head

00:03:16.359 --> 00:03:19.361
and move them together, one step at a time.

00:03:19.431 --> 00:03:21.595
They arrive at the first common

00:03:21.665 --> 00:03:23.340
node at the same moment.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.188
That takes at most M steps

00:03:26.188 --> 00:03:28.130
and no extra memory.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.668
When the lengths differ, we make them equal

00:03:32.668 --> 00:03:35.006
without copying anything.

00:03:35.006 --> 00:03:38.874
Imagine a longer list, L1′: list one

00:03:38.874 --> 00:03:40.853
followed by list two.

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:43.011
And L2′: list two

00:03:43.011 --> 00:03:44.990
followed by list one.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.537
Both have M + N nodes.

00:03:47.537 --> 00:03:50.963
And their last two nodes are the same

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:52.457
c1 and c2.

00:03:52.457 --> 00:03:54.653
So, by the special case

00:03:54.741 --> 00:03:58.607
two pointers moving together on L1′ and

00:03:58.695 --> 00:04:02.121
L2′ meet at the first common node

00:04:02.121 --> 00:04:05.460
here, c1, the eighth node of each.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.869
We never build L1′ or L2′.

00:04:08.869 --> 00:04:10.912
Pointer a walks list one

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.819
and when it reaches the end it jumps

00:04:13.898 --> 00:04:15.784
to the head of list two.

00:04:15.784 --> 00:04:17.827
Pointer b walks list two

00:04:17.827 --> 00:04:20.970
and then jumps to the head of list one.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.877
Before each step we compare a and b

00:04:23.955 --> 00:04:26.077
and we stop as soon as they

00:04:26.156 --> 00:04:27.570
are the same node.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.320
This is the version on the slides.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.267
Watch it on our lists.

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:36.494
a is at a1 and b at b1

00:04:36.494 --> 00:04:38.215
comparison 1.

00:04:38.215 --> 00:04:41.227
Both step: a2 and b2.

00:04:41.227 --> 00:04:43.271
a3 and c1.

00:04:43.271 --> 00:04:45.100
c1 and c2.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.551
Now b is at the end of list two

00:04:48.551 --> 00:04:52.815
so it jumps to the head of list one, a1

00:04:52.815 --> 00:04:55.141
while a moves to c2.

00:04:55.141 --> 00:04:59.404
Next, a reaches the end and jumps to b1

00:04:59.404 --> 00:05:01.730
while b moves to a2.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.763
b2 and a3.

00:05:03.763 --> 00:05:07.689
And now both are at c1: the same node

00:05:07.689 --> 00:05:10.680
found on the 8th comparison.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.800
Why does it work?

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.788
Say list one has x private nodes

00:05:15.879 --> 00:05:19.411
before the common part, list two has y

00:05:19.411 --> 00:05:22.399
and the common part has z nodes.

00:05:22.399 --> 00:05:26.022
Here x is 3, y is 2 and z is 2.

00:05:26.022 --> 00:05:29.373
So M is x + z, and N is y + z.

00:05:29.463 --> 00:05:32.995
Pointer a reaches the first common node

00:05:33.086 --> 00:05:36.346
after walking x + z + y nodes

00:05:36.346 --> 00:05:37.885
all of list one

00:05:37.885 --> 00:05:41.055
then the private part of list two.

00:05:41.055 --> 00:05:45.040
Pointer b reaches it after y + z + x.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.300
The same count, so the same moment.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.968
If x = y, they meet even earlier

00:05:51.968 --> 00:05:53.627
on the first pass.

00:05:53.715 --> 00:05:55.810
Either way the loop runs

00:05:55.898 --> 00:05:59.740
at most M + N times: O(M + N) time

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.010
and constant extra space.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.332
One catch.

00:06:03.406 --> 00:06:05.409
The slides' version assumes

00:06:05.483 --> 00:06:08.747
the lists do intersect, as the problem says.

00:06:08.747 --> 00:06:09.859
If they don't

00:06:09.859 --> 00:06:12.307
a and b are never the same node

00:06:12.307 --> 00:06:15.126
and the while true loop runs forever.

00:06:15.126 --> 00:06:17.870
We can fix that with a small change.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:21.436
The safe version loops while a is not b.

00:06:21.436 --> 00:06:23.675
It counts every pointer move.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.064
And a pointer at the end of its

00:06:26.139 --> 00:06:28.154
list first steps onto null

00:06:28.229 --> 00:06:30.095
and only on the next move

00:06:30.169 --> 00:06:31.961
jumps to the other head.

00:06:31.961 --> 00:06:33.528
When the loop stops

00:06:33.528 --> 00:06:36.290
a is the first common node, or null.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.781
If there is no common node

00:06:38.861 --> 00:06:40.843
both pointers become null

00:06:40.923 --> 00:06:42.430
at the same moment

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.888
after M + N + 1 moves

00:06:44.888 --> 00:06:47.664
so the loop ends and returns null.

00:06:47.664 --> 00:06:50.360
The cost is still O(M + N).

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.763
The demo runs all three on our lists.

00:06:53.839 --> 00:06:55.975
Method 1 finds c1 after

00:06:56.051 --> 00:06:57.577
15 comparisons.

00:06:57.577 --> 00:07:00.857
The slides' Method 2: c1 after 8.

00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:03.298
And the safe version also needs

00:07:03.375 --> 00:07:04.900
8 pointer moves.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.442
Then a list d1, d2

00:07:07.442 --> 00:07:10.598
which shares nothing with L1

00:07:10.598 --> 00:07:13.316
the safe version returns null.

00:07:13.404 --> 00:07:16.121
The slides' version would never

00:07:16.209 --> 00:07:18.050
have returned at all.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:22.839
Now you try, with the slides' Method 2.

00:07:22.839 --> 00:07:26.020
L1 is a1, then c1

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.971
c2 and c3.

00:07:27.971 --> 00:07:32.384
L2 is b1, b2, b3, b4

00:07:32.384 --> 00:07:34.950
and then the same c1.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.255
List the nodes that a visits

00:07:37.325 --> 00:07:40.396
until it meets b, and count the comparisons.

00:07:40.396 --> 00:07:42.700
Pause the video and work it out.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:51.215
Here x is 1, y is 4 and z is 3.

00:07:51.215 --> 00:07:56.520
Both start at their heads: comparison 1.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:00.565
a walks c1, c2 and c3

00:08:00.565 --> 00:08:03.364
while b walks to b4.

00:08:03.364 --> 00:08:07.730
Then a jumps to b1 and walks b2

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:12.320
b3 and b4, while b walks c1

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.350
c2, c3, and jumps to a1.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.969
On the next step both reach c1.

00:08:19.969 --> 00:08:22.757
They meet after 8 moves

00:08:22.757 --> 00:08:25.161
on the 9th comparison

00:08:25.161 --> 00:08:27.660
x + y + z + 1.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.055
To sum up.

00:08:29.055 --> 00:08:31.907
One: lists that share a node share

00:08:31.988 --> 00:08:35.247
everything after it, a Y and never an X

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:38.018
and common means the same object

00:08:38.018 --> 00:08:40.218
so compare the references.

00:08:40.218 --> 00:08:40.625
Two

00:08:40.625 --> 00:08:44.128
comparing every pair takes O(MN) time

00:08:44.128 --> 00:08:47.469
switching heads makes the lengths equal

00:08:47.551 --> 00:08:50.321
and finds the node in O(M + N) time

00:08:50.402 --> 00:08:53.091
with constant extra space.

00:08:53.091 --> 00:08:56.432
Three: the slides' version loops forever

00:08:56.513 --> 00:08:58.876
when there is no common node

00:08:58.876 --> 00:09:02.054
the safe version steps onto null once

00:09:02.054 --> 00:09:03.520
and returns null.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.788
Try exercise 2: count both lengths

00:09:06.867 --> 00:09:09.470
advance the pointer on the longer

00:09:09.548 --> 00:09:11.362
list by the difference

00:09:11.441 --> 00:09:13.965
then move both pointers together

00:09:14.044 --> 00:09:15.305
until they meet

00:09:15.305 --> 00:09:17.750
and explain why it is correct.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:21.275
Next, in part 4: cycles in a list

00:09:21.275 --> 00:09:24.020
and removing duplicate letters.

